@MadElf1337 - Thanks for updating the feature extractor tests. Regarding the currently failing tests, are you able to click on the `Details` link from the CI runs and see the CircleCI output? 

For tests like `check_code_quality` - you need to run `make fixup` in the top level of the repo. This is detailed in the doc @gante linked to previously: https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/add_tensorflow_model#debugging-mismatches-across-ml-frameworks. Make sure to look through this and go through all the steps to get the model ready. 

Do you have an estimation on when you will be able to address these failing tests and have the PR merge ready? BEiT is a very impactful model to add to our TensorFlow library, we'd be excited to include it and we're a lot closer with the work you've done :) This PR has been open for over 6 months now, so would be good to push to get it over the finish line.

@MadElf1337 I'm not sure why it's failing and I don't see it as a failed test on the CI runs. If it's the same imagenet error [posted here](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/pull/18559#issuecomment-1422953538): 
`AssertionError: TensorShape([1, 2]) != <tf.Tensor: shape=(2,), dtype=int32, numpy=array([   1, 1000], dtype=int32)>`

Then it seems it's a case of output shape. The shapes aren't wildly different, as one is a flattened version of the other. I would compare the shape of `pooled_outputs` before they're fed to the classifier between the pytorch beit and tensorflow beit models and work from there. If they're the same, then the issue is in the classifier. If they're different, keep going up the layers until you hit where the difference is arising.

@MadElf1337 

For the repo consistency tests, could you run `make fix-copies` and push those changes 

For the quality checks, could you run `make style` at the top level of the repo and push any changes? These should be run after running `make fix-copies`

@MadElf1337 Do you have the versions if black and ruff installed that correspond to the current versions expected by the library? 

The quality checks only fail if there are changes that would be made by running `make fix-copies` or `make style`. So by running the make commands, changes that would resolve the problem are made, rather than created. 

As I have stated many times before in this PR, please look at the errors on the CI runs. These will tell you what the issues are and how to resolve them. [I can see](https://app.circleci.com/pipelines/github/huggingface/transformers/68484/workflows/9d7a4249-3a4a-4fb5-a5c1-aeba5cf99fa8/jobs/857594) that the issue is arising in data2vec because `Dict` isn't defined.

@MadElf1337 OK, that's great. Now the fun begins! 

I can see from the CI run that one of the weights for the data2vec_vision model - pooper.layernorm.weight - is missing from the PyTorch model. This doesn't seem to be affecting the model outputs but will need to be resolved too. 

The difference at the moment is pretty small, thankfully! What I would do is check the max absolute difference between the TF and PT models' hidden states and activations to see if you can identify where the the difference is creeping in e.g. is it in the first block? From there, try to identify which operation / layer is introducing the error. From there, we can see whether it it irreducible or not.

@MadElf1337 All of the tests need to pass for the model to be merged. The equivalence tests are implemented in the [PT part of the library](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/f5c9738f61a4221e447bbf0c3a0496bd11583031/tests/test_modeling_common.py#L2087) so it's expected they would fail there when running cross tests.

> Also I tried using the models and passing images to them directly, but I'm not getting the desired output of activations and hidden states, maybe I'm doing something wrong there, can you help me a bit?

I might be able to help but would need some more information e.g. example code of what's being run and what the expected behaviour was.

@MadElf1337 Great - that provides a good place to work from. It's common to sometimes see large differences happening in the intermediate layers - it's most important the final activations have small differences. The difference between the models isn't huge ~6e-5, however there's still warnings in the tests about certain weights not being loaded in: 
`FAILED tests/models/data2vec/test_modeling_tf_data2vec_vision.py::TFData2VecVisionModelTest::test_pt_tf_model_equivalence - AttributeError: pooler.layernorm.weight not found in PyTorch model`

These should be addressed first. After that you can see if there's still significant differences between the models.

